The queue uses a Go slice as a circular array. `head` marks the front, while `count` tracks how many values are currently stored. New values are placed at `(head + count) % len(data)`.

When the slice is full, a larger slice is created and the current queue order is copied into it. This also makes the empty state unambiguous because `count` is zero.

```go
type ArrayQueue struct {
    data  []int
    head  int
    count int
}

func NewArrayQueue() *ArrayQueue {
    return &ArrayQueue{
        data: make([]int, 4),
    }
}

func (q *ArrayQueue) Push(x int) {
    if q.count == len(q.data) {
        expanded := make([]int, len(q.data)*2)
        for i := 0; i < q.count; i++ {
            expanded[i] = q.data[(q.head+i)%len(q.data)]
        }
        q.data = expanded
        q.head = 0
    }

    index := (q.head + q.count) % len(q.data)
    q.data[index] = x
    q.count++
}

func (q *ArrayQueue) Pop() int {
    answer := q.data[q.head]
    q.head = (q.head + 1) % len(q.data)
    q.count--
    return answer
}

func (q *ArrayQueue) Peek() int {
    return q.data[q.head]
}

func (q *ArrayQueue) IsEmpty() bool {
    return q.count == 0
}
```

With values `5, 6, 7`, popping returns `5` and moves `head` to the next position. Later pushes can reuse the slots at the beginning of the slice. Push, pop, peek, and IsEmpty are `O(1)` except for a resize, which costs `O(n)`. Space is `O(n)`.

