Here the front stays at index zero while the queue is active. A pop shifts the remaining values left. If the backing array fills up, it grows before adding the next value.

This is not the fastest version for many removals, but the state is very visible and it follows the array layout directly.

```java
class ArrayQueue {
    private int[] values;
    private int size;

    public ArrayQueue() {
        values = new int[4];
        size = 0;
    }

    public void push(int x) {
        if (size == values.length) {
            int[] expanded = new int[values.length * 2];
            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                expanded[i] = values[i];
            }
            values = expanded;
        }

        values[size] = x;
        size++;
    }

    public int pop() {
        int answer = values[0];

        for (int i = 1; i < size; i++) {
            values[i - 1] = values[i];
        }

        size--;
        return answer;
    }

    public int peek() {
        return values[0];
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }
}
```

For an empty queue, `isEmpty()` returns true because `size` is zero. After `push(2), push(4), pop()`, the remaining first value is moved into index zero, so `peek()` returns `4`.

`push()` is `O(1)` unless the array grows. `pop()` is `O(n)` because it shifts values, and `peek()` plus `isEmpty()` are `O(1)`. The array takes `O(n)` space.

This version treats the array as circular. The `front` index points to the next item to remove, and `size` tells where the next item should be inserted. When an index reaches the end, it wraps back to zero.

The array grows when it becomes full. During a resize, the values are copied in queue order, starting from the current front.

```java
class ArrayQueue {
    private int[] values;
    private int front;
    private int size;

    public ArrayQueue() {
        values = new int[4];
        front = 0;
        size = 0;
    }

    public void push(int x) {
        if (size == values.length) {
            int[] expanded = new int[values.length * 2];

            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                expanded[i] = values[(front + i) % values.length];
            }

            values = expanded;
            front = 0;
        }

        int back = (front + size) % values.length;
        values[back] = x;
        size++;
    }

    public int pop() {
        int answer = values[front];
        front = (front + 1) % values.length;
        size--;
        return answer;
    }

    public int peek() {
        return values[front];
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }
}
```

A sample run is `push(10)`, `push(20)`, `pop()`, `push(30)`. The first pop returns `10`, and `30` can use the free slot after the array wraps around. Normal operations are `O(1)`; a resize costs `O(n)`, but resizing happens only when the array is full. Space is `O(n)`.

A simple array-backed queue can keep the front at index `0`. New values go to the end, and `pop()` removes the first value by shifting the remaining values left.

For example, after `[4, 7, 9]`, popping removes `4` and changes the array to `[7, 9]`. This is easy to follow, but removing from the front costs linear time.

```cpp
#include <vector>

class ArrayQueue {
private:
    std::vector<int> values;

public:
    ArrayQueue() {
    }

    void push(int x) {
        values.push_back(x);
    }

    int pop() {
        int frontValue = values[0];
        values.erase(values.begin());
        return frontValue;
    }

    int peek() {
        return values[0];
    }

    bool isEmpty() {
        return values.empty();
    }
};
```

`push()` and `isEmpty()` take `O(1)` time. `peek()` is `O(1)`, while `pop()` is `O(n)` because the array elements are shifted. The stored values use `O(n)` space. The input is stored inside the array and is changed when an item is removed.

